In this lesson we'll look at combining fractional indices, and the good news is that all five index laws still work exactly the same — even when the exponents are fractions or negative numbers.

The key strategy is to first rewrite every term so it has the same base if you can. For example, four to the one-half times sixteen to the one-quarter both become two to some power, which makes them easy to combine. Once the bases match, you just apply the laws to add, subtract, or multiply the exponents. Then if the question asks for a surd form, you convert the answer back at the end.

Now, a couple of rules that still hold even with fractions. A negative fractional index means you flip the term, just like a whole-number negative index. And anything to the power of zero equals one, even when zero came from fractional exponents. So the square root of a times a to the negative one-half gives you a to the power of one-half minus one-half, which is a to the zero, and that equals one. Pretty neat, right?

Now let's see it in action.